## §9 Parent-event alias stash

PoD's duel-launcher parents (the events that call
`configure_start_single_combat_effect`) routinely save custom scope
aliases like `scope:spirit`, `scope:my_rival`, `scope:scheme` that
their follow-up events read after combat resolves. Under vanilla's
duel chain those scopes propagate through the event graph. The
minigame's scripted-GUI execute boundary breaks that chain, so
`pod_combat_sc_stash_parent_aliases_effect` captures each alias as a
story var on the `POD_combat_sc_context` story at launch, and
`pod_combat.9999` rebuilds them as saved scopes before firing the
parent's follow-up.

The whitelist below was audited across every PoD caller of
`configure_start_single_combat_effect` as of v1.18.3.1 integration.
Each row stashed as `alias_<name>` on the sc_context story.

### Character aliases

| Alias | Source |
|---|---|
| `spirit` | PoD spirit-combat events (umbra, fera, demon hunts) |
| `alastor` | PoD wraith Shadow / Spectre events |
| `wyrm_champion` | PoD Wyrm-corruption duels |
| `vampire_attacker` | PoD vampire predation events |
| `actor` | Vanilla murder / scheme actor |
| `parent` | Vanilla event-parent reference |
| `aggressor_vassal` | Vanilla rebellion / liege-vassal duels |
| `distant_knight` | Vanilla tournament chains |
| `snitch` | Vanilla espionage event chain |
| `strong_leader` | Vanilla strongman / coup events |
| `warrior_monk` | Vanilla monastic combat events |
| `attacked_rival` | Vanilla rivalry duel chain |
| `my_rival` | Vanilla rival-related event branches |
| `rival_burner` | Vanilla rivalry retaliation |
| `rival_on_sight` | Vanilla rival-meeting confrontations |
| `rivals_child` | Vanilla rival's-child duel chains |
| `og_root` | Vanilla "original root" preservation pattern |
| `root_for_loc` | Vanilla loc-substitution character ref |
| `recidivist` | Vanilla crime / recidivism chain |
| `owner` | Scheme owner / action initiator. Widely used by vanilla `murder_success_kill_effect` and its callees, which pass `MURDERER = scope:owner` down into `unknown_murder_effect` / `pod_death`. Missing this cascades into `scope:pod_killer` errors because `pod_death` does `$killer$ = { save_temporary_scope_as = pod_killer }` and `$killer$` resolves from it. |

### Artifact aliases

| Alias | Source |
|---|---|
| `shadowflame` | PoD shadowflame artifact events |
| `burned_artifact` | PoD artifact-destruction chain |

### Scheme aliases

| Alias | Source |
|---|---|
| `scheme` | `POD_diablerize.600` runs `end_scheme = scope:scheme` after the duel resolves; without this stash, its option tooltips throw "Undefined event target 'scheme'" and the final `end_scheme` call fails. |

### Title aliases

| Alias | Source |
|---|---|
| `vassal_claim` | Vanilla `hold_court.8210` (vassal-rebellion legal-duel chain) saves the contested title as `scope:vassal_claim` before the duel; `hold_court.8211` reads it twice in the follow-up to transfer the title from loser to `scope:sc_victor`. PC can be either party (`aggressor_vassal` / `defender_vassal`), so the minigame intercepts. |

### Adding a new alias

When a new parent event surfaces an undefined-event-target error in
its follow-up, add to `pod_combat_sc_stash_parent_aliases_effect`:

```
if = { limit = { exists = scope:<name> } scope:sc_ctx = { set_variable = { name = alias_<name> value = scope:<name> } } }
```

…and the matching restore line in `pod_combat.9999`'s alias-rebuild
block. The whitelist is open by design — `exists =` guards mean
extra branches that find no scope at launch silently skip.

### Surfaces NOT yet stashed

- **Typed scope objects**: `scope:vassal_war`, `scope:vassal_claim`,
  `scope:laamp_title`. The variable-storage pattern should work the
  same way — add when triggered in testing.
- **Raw scope values**: `scope:duel_legal`, `scope:fp2_*_gate`. These
  aren't character or object scopes; would need a different detection
  mechanism than `exists = scope:X`.
